<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pristina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Priština</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pristina is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a market town near the mining centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepca">Trepča</a> and a residence of Serbian rulers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> in the late 14th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> of 1389 was fought nearby. Under Ottoman rule from 1455, it became the administrative centre of Kosovo in the 20th century and capital of the independent state in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pristina.jpg" alt="Pristina" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IEstiv03 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Prishtina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pristina lies on an alluvial plain in the regions of Llap and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, across the Gollak Hills in central and eastern Kosovo. The area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and the earliest recognised traces were found at Gračanica, Matiçan and Ulpiana. In antiquity it belonged to the Dardanian Kingdom, and the nearby settlement of Ulpiana represents the heritage of the classical era. The origin of the city's name is unknown; Eric P. Hamp connected it with an Indo-European word for a ford and a word cognate with English stone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the area stayed within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> between the 5th and 9th centuries. It was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> in the middle of the 9th century, fell again to the Byzantine Empire in the early 11th century, when it was part of a theme called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and passed several times to the Second Bulgarian Empire. The Kingdom, later Empire, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> held it through the 13th and 14th centuries. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a> founded the nearby Gračanica monastery in 1315, and Pristina is first recorded in 1342, as a village, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>.</p><p>In 1455 it had a small population of 300 households. In the following century it became an important mining and trading centre because of its position near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a>, known for its trade fairs and for goatskin, goat hair and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. During the Austro-Turkish War of the late 17th century, townspeople under the Catholic Albanian priest Pjetër Bogdani pledged loyalty to the Austrian army, and he supplied a force of 6,000 Albanian soldiers. According to Noel Malcolm the town then had a majority of about 15,000 Muslims, probably Albanian.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> pillaged and partly burned Pristina in May 1901. Serb forces took it on 22 October 1912, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> occupied it in 1915. In 1948 it was chosen as the capital of the province of SAP Kosovo within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and it remained the capital after Kosovo declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pristina is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, with 227,466 residents at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2024, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>. It holds the Parliament, the courts and the residences of the president and prime minister. The University of Pristina, founded in the 20th century, is the largest and oldest in the city, and the National Theatre of Kosovo dates from 1946. Of 426 protected monuments in Kosovo, 21 are in Pristina, many of them from the Byzantine and Ottoman periods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pristina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the First Balkan War it took <b>Pristina</b> and Novi Pazar, defeated the Ottoman army at the Battle of Kumanovo and conquered Skopje and the Kosovo vilayet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1371 he held <b>Priština</b>, Peć and the mining districts, and later took Skopje, ruling as Lazar's ally.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-brankovic">District of Branković</a></li></ul></section>
